  "exploiter_output": {
    "evidence": "Root cause / sink: src/main/java/org/codehaus/plexus/util/cli/shell/Shell.java:149 \u2014 `sb.append(StringUtils.quoteAndEscape(getExecutable(), getExecutableQuoteDelimiter(), escapeChars, getQuotingTriggerChars(), '\\\\', false))` where escapeChars is empty (isSingleQuotedExecutableEscaped()/isDoubleQuotedExecutableEscaped() both false by default), so quoteAndEscape (src/main/java/org/codehaus/plexus/util/StringUtils.java:2303) only wraps the string in a quote character when a \"trigger\" char (space, $, ;, &, |, (, ), etc. \u2014 BourneShell.BASH_QUOTING_TRIGGER_CHARS) is present; it never escapes those metacharacters. For BourneShell specifically, getExecutable() (src/main/java/org/codehaus/plexus/util/cli/shell/BourneShell.java:69-76) additionally routes the value through unifyQuotes() -> StringUtils.quoteAndEscape(path, '\"', BASH_QUOTING_TRIGGER_CHARS), which only escapes literal `\"` characters. The resulting string (double-quoted, metacharacters intact) is concatenated into one command string (Shell.java:153/175, CmdShell.java:80-87 for cmd.exe) that Commandline.execute() passes as the last element of the String[] array to BOTH Runtime.exec overloads: Commandline.java:636 `Runtime.getRuntime().exec(getShellCommandline(), environment)` and Commandline.java:651 `Runtime.getRuntime().exec(getShellCommandline(), environment, workingDir)`. Trust boundary: any caller passing a not-fully-trusted string into Commandline#setExecutable(String)/Commandline(String) (build tool name, user-configured path/version fragment, plugin parameter, etc.) crosses into this sink because double-quote wrapping does not block POSIX command substitution ($(...) / backticks). Every distinct sink/source path the alert enumerates that a complete fix must cover: (1) Shell.java:149 executable quoting via quoteAndEscape (POV target \u2014 shortest, cleanest path). (2) Shell.java:167 argument quoting via quoteAndEscape(arguments[i], ...) \u2014 arguments are wrapped in single quotes by BourneShell (safer) but in double quotes/no quoting for other Shell subclasses/configurations, and the fallback branch at Shell.java:171 (isQuotedArgumentsEnabled()==false) appends the raw argument with no quoting/escaping at all. (3) CmdShell.getCommandLine (src/main/java/org/codehaus/plexus/util/cli/shell/CmdShell.java:80-87) which wraps the already-built executable+argument string in one extra pair of double quotes for cmd.exe \u2014 same command-substitution-in-quotes class of issue on Windows via cmd.exe's own quote-stripping rules. (4) Both Runtime.exec call sites, Commandline.java:636 and Commandline.java:651 (with/without workingDir) \u2014 a fix applied to only one leaves the other exploitable. (5) The deprecated/back-compat chain (Commandline.java:501/506/622/674/700/702, getShellCommandline()/getShellCommandLine()) which funnels the same tainted executable/arguments to the same two exec sinks.",
    "expected_vulnerable_behavior": "On unpatched code, Commandline.setExecutable(\"true$(touch &lt;marker&gt;)\") followed by cmd.execute() causes BourneShell to build the shell line [\"/bin/sh\", \"-c\", \"\\\"true$(touch &lt;marker&gt;)\\\"\"] (visible in the printed \"Shell command line: ...\" line). Runtime.exec() invokes /bin/sh -c with that string; POSIX command substitution executes even inside the double quotes, so `touch &lt;marker&gt;` runs as a side effect before the (now-mangled) \"true \" program lookup succeeds/fails. The Java harness polls for the marker file and calls System.exit(0) when it appears, printing \"VULNERABLE: injected shell command executed; marker created at ...\". After a correct fix (e.g. rejecting/escaping shell metacharacters in the executable, or using single-quote wrapping that fully neutralizes $(...)), the marker file is never created, the harness prints \"NOT VULNERABLE...\" and calls System.exit(1), which propagates as a non-zero exit from the shell command (`&&` chain).",
    "notes": "Targeted alert trace: vulnerability group 1 / trace 1 (and the analogous trace 1/2 in group 2, which differ only in whether getShellCommandline() is reached via the deprecated Commandline.java:622/651 path or the\u76f4\u63a5 :636/506 path) \u2014 the \"executable\" flow: Shell.java:320/326 setExecutable -> Commandline.java:674/702 shell.executable -> Shell.java:149/153 quoteAndEscape -> CmdShell/BourneShell command-line assembly -> Commandline.java:636 and :651 Runtime.exec. Chose this over the argument-based traces (group 1 traces 2-3, group 2 traces 3-4, which go through Shell.java:167/171 quoteAndEscape on `arguments[]`, optionally via StringUtils.java:2303/2317) because: (1) it is the shared root cause \u2014 the same quoteAndEscape-with-empty-escape-set pattern \u2014 reached via the fewest hops, requiring only Commandline.setExecutable() with no argument manipulation; (2) BourneShell quotes arguments with single quotes (safe against $()) by default, so demonstrating the argument path would require disabling quoting or targeting CmdShell/Windows, which isn't the container's OS (Linux/arm64, confirmed via `docker run` and BourneShell selected by Commandline's setDefaultShell() since Os.isFamily(WINDOWS) is false); the executable path is vulnerable unconditionally on this OS with default settings. Verified live in the project container: printed shell command line was `[/bin/sh, -c, \"true$(touch /tmp/plexus_pov_markerNNN.txt)\"]` and the marker file was created, confirming genuine command execution through the real Commandline/Shell/Runtime.exec code path (not simulated). The CmdShell (Windows cmd.exe) and raw-argument-quoting paths are recorded above as required coverage for the patcher even though this POV does not execute them directly, since this container/OS cannot exercise the Windows-specific CmdShell branch.",
    "pov_command": "mvn -q -B -DskipTests compile && mkdir -p .security-pipeline/pov/classes && javac -cp target/classes -d .security-pipeline/pov/classes .security-pipeline/pov/CommandInjectionPov.java && java -cp target/classes:.security-pipeline/pov/classes CommandInjectionPov",
